<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matteo I Visconti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1250">1250</a>–<a href="/years/1322">1322</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matteo I Visconti was lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and the real founder of the Visconti state. Great-nephew of Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottone-visconti">Ottone Visconti</a>, he was named imperial vicar in 1294, was driven out by the della Torre in 1302 and returned with the support of Emperor Henry VII in 1311. A Ghibelline leader, he extended Milanese power over much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> but was excommunicated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matteo-i-visconti.jpg" alt="Matteo I Visconti" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portret_van_Matteo_Visconti,_RP-P-1874-12-11-1_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Matteo was the son of Teobaldo Visconti and Anastasia Pirovano. In August 1269 he married Bonacossa Borri, with whom he had many children, among them Galeazzo I, Luchino, Marco, Stefano and Giovanni, later archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. His great-uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottone-visconti">Ottone Visconti</a>, archbishop and first lord of Milan, appointed him Captain of the People, and the citizens re-elected him to the office in 1289 and 1294.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the death of William VII, Marquess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>, in 1292, Matteo took Casale and the lordships of Novara and Vercelli, though John of Monferrat soon drove the Ghibellines out again. Ottone's death in 1295 reopened the struggle between the Ghibellines and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelphs</a>, led by the Della Torre family. In June 1302 a league of the Torriani and hostile cities, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberto-scotti">Alberto Scotti</a> and Ghiberto da Correggio, gave the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> to Guido della Torre; Matteo's house was looted and he spent several years in exile as a guest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaliger</a> family.</p><p>In 1311 he met King Henry VII at Asti. After Henry's soldiers defeated Guido della Torre in February, Henry sold Matteo the title of imperial vicar for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 13 July 1311, and a league of Ghibelline cities followed, among them Como, Bergamo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>, Cremona and Piacenza. Matteo had little military talent, but in October 1315 his sons Marco and Luchino defeated the Tuscan Guelphs on the Scrivia River near Voghera and took Pavia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1317 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a>, in the bull Si fratrum, threatened with excommunication anyone who used the title of imperial vicar without papal consent, and Matteo took instead the title of General Lord of the Milanese People. The papal legate, Cardinal Bertrand du Poujet, proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the Visconti. In 1322 the pope charged Matteo with necromancy, and he was convicted in absentia after refusing to come to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>; the archbishop Aicardo da Camodeia condemned him and Galeazzo as heretics. At the end of May 1322 Matteo handed power to Galeazzo and retired to Crescenzago. He died on 24 June 1322.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matteo I Visconti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The della Torre were challenged by Archbishop Ottone Visconti, who defeated them at Desio in 1277; his great-nephew <b>Matteo I Visconti</b> received the title of imperial vicar in 1294.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-milan">Lordship of Milan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Invorio, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Crescenzago, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.75,8.48&amp;year=1296" aria-label="Invorio on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4751 551 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Matteo I Visconti"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5201 821h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5201 821h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.75,8.48&amp;year=1296">What was here in 1296? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
